<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">opinions</ansi>

Your companions watch what you choose, and they have views. When you spare
a beaten foe, pay for a bed, share your own bread or sell a relic, the
companions with you may say what they think, in one line, and their
loyalty moves a little.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Reading what they think</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">opinions</ansi>             every companion: temperament, mood, what each likes and
                      dislikes, and what each said lately
  <ansi fg="command">opinions [member]</ansi>     one companion
  <ansi fg="command">company inspect [member]</ansi>  one line of the same, with the rest of their record

In the web client the Company window has an Opinions tab with the same
cards. It fits a phone as it does the desktop. Reading it costs nothing,
even in a fight.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What they weigh</ansi>

Each companion's temperament (stoic, cheerful, grim, boastful, wry or
devout; <ansi fg="command">help banter</ansi>) likes some of these and dislikes others:

  mercy to the beaten             you spared a surrendered foe (<ansi fg="command">help mercy</ansi>)
  executing the beaten            you did not
  a bed at an inn                 you paid for rooms (<ansi fg="command">help inn</ansi>)
  a rough camp                    you rested in the wild (<ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>)
  sharing your own food           you fed or watered a companion from your own
                                  pack, not the company's cargo (<ansi fg="command">help company meal</ansi>)
  selling relics                  you sold a relic for coin, by <ansi fg="command">sell</ansi> or <ansi fg="command">sell junk</ansi>
                                  (<ansi fg="command">help relics</ansi>)
  kindness, greed, courage,       the stance of a choice in a scene on the road
  prudence, reverence, cunning    (<ansi fg="command">help events</ansi>)

A temperament has a view on seven or eight of these and none on the rest.
A devout companion likes mercy, kindness, reverence and sharing, and
dislikes executions, selling relics, greed and lies. A grim one likes
executions, a rough camp and caution, and dislikes mercy and soft beds.
Read <ansi fg="command">opinions</ansi> for your own company.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What it does</ansi>

  - A companion who agrees gains 2 loyalty; one who disagrees loses 2.
    Loyalty is what keeps a companion with you (<ansi fg="command">help alignment</ansi>).
  - Opinions only nudge loyalty. Approval never lifts it above 80, and
    disapproval never takes it below 30, so a companion is never driven off
    or made to waver in a fight by your opinions alone.
  - A companion speaks once for each choice, and again about the same kind
    of choice only after a while: ten minutes for mercy and scenes, half an
    hour for selling a relic, two hours for camps, inns and food. Repeating
    a choice does not farm loyalty.
  - Mercy already moves companions by their alignment (<ansi fg="command">help mercy</ansi>). One
    that alignment moved does not speak twice; the others answer by
    temperament.
  - The company's creatures and constructs, the fallen and anyone away from
    you do not weigh in.

It gives no gold, experience or power, and it never moves the world's clock.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help company</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help banter</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help alignment</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help mercy</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help chronicle</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help webclient</ansi>.
